 \begin{abstract}
-Detailed balance removes directional cycle flow from a Markov model, trading estimation variance against restriction bias. We evaluate this trade-off for next-session open-to-close return terciles in SPY, QQQ and IWM, using 2,766 forecasts per asset over 2015--2025 and a fixed intraday allocation rule. The unrestricted model has the lowest point-estimate log loss and Brier score for each asset. Across six log-loss contrasts, Romano--Wolf adjusted $p$-values for unrestricted versus IID are 0.093, 0.008 and 0.048; IWM is significant at 5\% under stepdown but sensitive to the adjustment method. No primary reversible-minus-unrestricted log-loss interval excludes zero. Exact log-loss factorization places most SPY and QQQ improvement in middle-versus-outer terciles, to which the allocation is locally less sensitive; IWM also improves conditional upper-versus-lower prediction. Mean net gains over IID are 0.043, 0.020 and 0.073 basis points per day, while maintaining near-half exposure costs about five basis points per day under daily liquidation. All nine allocations lose capital at that cost, although their incremental cost differences are small. The unrestricted log-loss advantage is strongest in 2015--2018 and reverses for SPY and QQQ in 2023--2025. The study integrates forecast decomposition, decision sensitivity and cost accounting into a diagnostic template for modest forecasting gains. It demonstrates no forecast benefit from detailed balance in this design and does not evaluate its economic value under turnover-aware alternatives.
+Detailed balance removes directional cycle flow from a Markov model, trading estimation variance against restriction bias. We evaluate this trade-off for next-session open-to-close return terciles in SPY, QQQ and IWM, using 2,766 forecasts per asset over 2015--2025 and a fixed intraday allocation rule. The unrestricted model has the lowest point-estimate log loss and Brier score for each asset. Across six log-loss contrasts, Romano--Wolf adjusted $p$-values for unrestricted versus IID are 0.093, 0.008 and 0.048; IWM is significant at 5\% under stepdown but sensitive to the adjustment method. No primary reversible-minus-unrestricted log-loss interval excludes zero. Exact log-loss factorization places most SPY and QQQ improvement in middle-versus-outer terciles, to which the allocation is locally less sensitive; IWM also improves conditional upper-versus-lower prediction. Mean net gains over IID are 0.043, 0.020 and 0.073 basis points per day, while daily liquidation of a near-half exposure costs about five basis points per session, a baseline common to all nine allocations, which therefore all lose capital; their incremental cost differences are below 0.005 basis points per day. The unrestricted log-loss advantage is strongest in 2015--2018 and reverses for SPY and QQQ in 2023--2025. The study integrates forecast decomposition, decision sensitivity and cost accounting into a diagnostic template for modest forecasting gains. It demonstrates no forecast benefit from detailed balance in this design and does not evaluate its economic value under turnover-aware alternatives.
 \end{abstract}
 

